&lt;h1&gt;


MERNStack #WebDevelopment #FullStackDeveloper #JavaScript #ReactJS #NodeJS #ExpressJS #MongoDB #BuildInPublic #LearnToCode #CodingJourney #SoftwareEngineering #JuniorDeveloper
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;// server.js&lt;br&gt;
import "dotenv/config";&lt;br&gt;
import express from "express";&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;const app = express();&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;app.get("/conversation-token", async (req, res) =&amp;gt; {&lt;br&gt;
const response = await fetch(&lt;br&gt;
&lt;code&gt;https://api.elevenlabs.io/v1/convai/conversation/token?agent_id=${process.env.AGENT_ID}&lt;/code&gt;,&lt;br&gt;
{ headers: { "xi-api-key": process.env.ELEVENLABS_API_KEY } }&lt;br&gt;
);&lt;br&gt;
if (!response.ok) return res.status(500).send("Failed to get conversation token");&lt;br&gt;
const body = await response.json();&lt;br&gt;
res.send(body.token);&lt;br&gt;
});&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;app.listen(3001);&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A small UI change should be simple to explain. But when working with coding agents, I kept spending time describing which button, heading, or card I meant.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That’s why I built &lt;strong&gt;Noktiq&lt;/strong&gt;, a visual feedback tool for React and Vue.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Point. Note. Paste.
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Noktiq runs on your development website:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Select the element you want to change.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Write a note describing the change.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Copy the request into your existing coding agent.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When available, the request includes the &lt;strong&gt;source file, line, and column&lt;/strong&gt;, alongside a selector and page context.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Noktiq gathers the context. Your coding agent makes the code changes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F943s6cup758c359t87im.gif" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F943s6cup758c359t87im.gif" alt="Noktiq selection and annotation workflow" width="560" height="376"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What does the copied request look like?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of describing an element’s position from memory, you can give your agent something like this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Source: components/sections/About/About.tsx:148:17
Page: http://localhost:3000/tr
Selector: #about a
Visible text: "View my CV"
Requested change: "Change the text to Download my CV."
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This is a shortened example. Actual selectors may be more specific, especially when similar elements appear more than once.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Source locations point to the code that renders the element. They don't necessarily identify the CSS rule, translation file, or event handler that needs editing, so the agent still needs to verify the target.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


React and Vue, the same workflow
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Noktiq supports:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Individual elements, selected text, multiple elements, and area selection.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Numbered pins, target status, and rebinding notes to another element.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Copying individual notes or all notes together.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Compact, Standard, and Detailed output.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A draggable mascot and local browser note storage.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Documented integrations include Next.js, Nuxt, Vite, and Webpack. Compatibility depends on the framework version and project setup; the repository includes tested configurations and limitations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Getting started
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npm &lt;span class="nb"&gt;install&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-D&lt;/span&gt; noktiq
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Installation alone doesn't display the toolbar. Add the React or Vue component and configure the source integration using the &lt;a href="https://github.com/DuranGZR/Noktiq#usage" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;setup guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep the documented development condition so the toolbar stays hidden in production.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Noktiq doesn't require an AI account or API key, and it doesn't send your notes to an AI service. You review and copy the request into the coding tool you already use.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why I’m building it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My goal is to reduce repeated explanations and unnecessary code searches when making visual changes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That could also reduce token usage across a task, but I haven't measured the savings yet. A structured request can be longer than a short prompt—the potential benefit comes from avoiding clarification and correction rounds afterward.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Noktiq is available on npm, and the source code is on GitHub under the PolyForm Shield 1.0.0 license.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/DuranGZR/Noktiq" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub and documentation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/noktiq" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;npm package&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'd love to hear your feedback. Would this fit into your workflow, and what would make it more useful?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


Optimizing UI Updates for Performance
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Introduction&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React is known for its efficient rendering process, and one of the key techniques it utilizes is batching. Batching refers to the process of combining multiple&lt;br&gt;
updates into a single update, minimizing the number of times the real DOM needs to be updated. In this article, we will explore how batching works under the&lt;br&gt;
hood in React and its impact on performance. We will also provide examples to demonstrate the concept.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Understanding Batching&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When React renders a component, it doesn’t immediately apply every change to the real DOM. Instead, it batches the updates together and performs them all at&lt;br&gt;
once, resulting in better performance and a smoother user experience. React achieves batching through a technique called “transaction,” which groups related&lt;br&gt;
operations and executes them as a single unit of work.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Example: Updating State with Batching:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let’s consider an example where a component updates its state multiple times in quick succession. Without batching, React would update the real DOM for each&lt;br&gt;
state change separately, even if the actual value changed only once. This approach would be inefficient.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, due to batching, React combines these updates into a single transaction. As a result, the real DOM is updated only once after all the state changes&lt;br&gt;
have been processed. This reduces unnecessary DOM updates and improves performance.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To demonstrate this, let’s modify the example code to log the current transaction state:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;Example&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;handleClick&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;before &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;__SECRET_INTERNALS_DO_NOT_USE_OR_YOU_WILL_BE_FIRED&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ReactCurrentOwner&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;currentDispatcher&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;after &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;__SECRET_INTERNALS_DO_NOT_USE_OR_YOU_WILL_BE_FIRED&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ReactCurrentOwner&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;currentDispatcher&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;Count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;/p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;&amp;gt;
&lt;/span&gt;            &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;onClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;handleClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;Increment&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;/button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;&amp;gt;
&lt;/span&gt;        &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;/div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;&amp;gt;
&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;In this modified example, we log the current transaction state before and after the state updates. By observing the logs, we can see that React batches the&lt;br&gt;
updates together into a single transaction.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Common Pitfall: Event Handlers and Batching: React’s batching behavior can sometimes lead to confusion, especially when dealing with event handlers. Consider an&lt;br&gt;
example where a component updates its state and logs the count immediately after the state update. Due to batching, the logged count will always be one less&lt;br&gt;
than the actual value at that moment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This happens because React schedules a batch of updates to the real DOM, deferring the actual application of changes. Therefore, when the log statement is&lt;br&gt;
executed, the state update might not have been processed yet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fiber Architecture and Incremental Updates&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React Fiber, introduced in React 16, is a re-implemented rendering engine that offers enhanced efficiency and flexibility. It introduces incremental updates to&lt;br&gt;
the virtual DOM, enabling the browser to handle user input and other events without blocking the UI.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;With React Fiber, updates are broken down into smaller chunks, allowing for prioritization and interleaving of different tasks. This approach enhances&lt;br&gt;
responsiveness and ensures that the user interface remains interactive even during intense rendering processes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Conclusion&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Batching is a fundamental mechanism in React that optimizes UI updates for improved performance. By combining multiple updates into a single transaction, React&lt;br&gt;
minimizes the number of DOM manipulations and delivers a smoother user experience. Understanding batching and its impact on event handling and performance is&lt;br&gt;
crucial for developing efficient React applications.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In this article, we discussed the concept of batching in React, its implementation through transactions, and its benefits in terms of performance. We provided&lt;br&gt;
examples to illustrate how batching works and explained the significance of React Fiber’s incremental updates. By leveraging batching effectively, developers&lt;br&gt;
can build highly performant and responsive React applications.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Thank you for reading! ❤️&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You want to check how a hook behaves. The usual route is a new project: scaffold, install, start a dev server, delete the boilerplate. That's a lot of ceremony for twenty lines.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today's twenty lines: a &lt;code&gt;useDebouncedValue&lt;/code&gt; hook, the kind you write for a search box so it doesn't filter on every keystroke.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The hook
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useDebouncedValue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;T&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;T&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ms&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;250&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;T&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;debounced&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setDebounced&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setTimeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setDebounced&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ms&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;clearTimeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ms&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;debounced&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;How it works: every time &lt;code&gt;value&lt;/code&gt; changes, the effect starts a timer. If &lt;code&gt;value&lt;/code&gt; changes again before the timer fires, React runs the cleanup first, which cancels the old timer. So &lt;code&gt;debounced&lt;/code&gt; only catches up once the input has been still for &lt;code&gt;ms&lt;/code&gt; milliseconds.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two details that bite people:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The cleanup is the debounce.&lt;/strong&gt; Without &lt;code&gt;return () =&amp;gt; clearTimeout(id)&lt;/code&gt;, every keystroke schedules an update and you've built a delay, not a debounce.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;ms&lt;/code&gt; belongs in the dependency array.&lt;/strong&gt; Leave it out and changing the delay does nothing until &lt;code&gt;value&lt;/code&gt; changes too.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A component to try it in
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useMemo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// useDebouncedValue from above goes here&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fruits&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;apple&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;apricot&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;banana&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;blueberry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;cherry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;grape&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;mango&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;];&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;App&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setQuery&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;debounced&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useDebouncedValue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;300&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;matches&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useMemo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fruits&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;includes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;debounced&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toLowerCase&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;())),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;debounced&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;style&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;fontFamily&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;system-ui&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;padding&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;24&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;input&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;onChange&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setQuery&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;target&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;placeholder&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"Filter fruit"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;matches&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; of &lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;fruits&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; (typed: "&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;", filtering: "&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;debounced&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;")
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;ul&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;matches&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;li&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;li&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;ul&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Showing both &lt;code&gt;query&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;debounced&lt;/code&gt; on screen is the trick: type fast and you can watch the second one lag behind, then snap into place.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Running it without a project
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I ran this in JSLab, a JavaScript &amp;amp; TypeScript scratchpad for macOS. Click &lt;strong&gt;+&lt;/strong&gt; in the tab bar and pick &lt;strong&gt;React&lt;/strong&gt;: JSLab sets the tab's language, runtime and framework, installs the packages it needs, and opens starter code. Paste the file over it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;JSLab renders it into a &lt;strong&gt;Web View&lt;/strong&gt; tile beside the console. Edit the JSX and it rerenders as you type. Change &lt;code&gt;300&lt;/code&gt; to &lt;code&gt;1000&lt;/code&gt; and you feel the difference on the next keystroke.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's a smaller component running the same way in a real JSLab Browser tab: a &lt;code&gt;Counter&lt;/code&gt; mounted with &lt;code&gt;createRoot&lt;/code&gt;, ticking in the Web View beside the code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F14wdicpu6yl9df3m3dd4.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F14wdicpu6yl9df3m3dd4.png" alt="A JSLab Browser tab set to TSX and React. The code defines a Counter component with useState and useEffect, ticking every 500 ms, and mounts it with createRoot. The Web View on the right shows Ticks: 24." width="800" height="450"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The console still works alongside it. Add a line like this inside the effect and the logs land in the output panel, tagged with their line:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;debounced →&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Want styling? Turn on &lt;strong&gt;Tailwind&lt;/strong&gt; in the status bar and the utility classes your code uses get compiled into the page, with nothing to install.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


When to make it a real project
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A scratchpad is for the question "does this hook behave the way I think?" Once the answer is yes, move it into your app with its tests. The point is not having to pay for a project before you know you need one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;JSLab launches Nov 15. The Web View and framework docs are here: &lt;a href="https://js-lab.app/docs/web-view" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://js-lab.app/docs/web-view&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This post was created with AI assistance and reviewed for accuracy before publishing.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If your production console shows &lt;code&gt;Minified React error #418&lt;/code&gt;, React is telling you that the HTML the server sent and the first render in the browser did not match. The development build spells it out ("Hydration failed because the server rendered HTML didn't match the client") and shows a diff. The production build gives you a number and a link to &lt;code&gt;react.dev/errors/418&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It is easy to treat it as console noise, because the page still works. It is not free. When hydration fails, React throws away the server-rendered tree and renders the whole root again in the browser. On my site that one error took mobile Total Blocking Time from 0 to 420 ms and the Lighthouse performance score from 96 to 83, and pushed Largest Contentful Paint 1.2 seconds past First Contentful Paint, which had always been equal.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I have shipped this error five different ways on one Next.js site. Here is each one, how it was found, and the fix that stuck.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


1. Randomness or time in a &lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt; initializer
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The games on the site generate a puzzle, a word or a board. The obvious code seeds state when the component is created:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Wrong: the initializer runs on the server and again in the browser.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;puzzle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setPuzzle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;generatePuzzle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;());&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The initializer runs twice, once during prerendering and once during hydration, and &lt;code&gt;Math.random()&lt;/code&gt; gives a different answer each time. The same thing happened in a lorem ipsum generator, a mock data generator and a password generator. Anything that reads &lt;code&gt;Date.now()&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;new Date()&lt;/code&gt; while rendering has the same problem.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fix is a deterministic first value, with the random one set after mount:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;puzzle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setPuzzle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;INITIAL_PUZZLE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// fixed, the same everywhere&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setPuzzle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;generatePuzzle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;());&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// runs only in the browser, after hydration&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[]);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The first frame shows the fixed puzzle for an instant. For a game that is fine. For anything where the flash matters, render a placeholder of the same size until the effect runs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


2. Formatting with the machine's locale
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Wrong&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;span&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;downloads&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toLocaleString&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;span&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toLocaleDateString&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;With no locale argument, &lt;code&gt;toLocaleString()&lt;/code&gt; uses whatever the current machine prefers. The server that prerenders the page (on Vercel, en-US in UTC) and a visitor in Germany or India produce different strings, so the text differs and hydration fails. It never reproduces on your own machine if your machine matches the server.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Pass the locale you rendered with, or move the formatting after mount:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;span&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;downloads&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toLocaleString&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;en-US&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;span&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;To catch this class of bug, load the deployed page in a browser with a different locale and time zone. In Playwright that is two options on the context:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;browser&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;newContext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;locale&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;de-DE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;timezoneId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;America/Los_Angeles&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


3. Browser-only APIs while rendering
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Five tools on the site called something during render that only exists, or only behaves the same, in a browser: &lt;code&gt;DOMParser&lt;/code&gt; in an XPath evaluator, &lt;code&gt;document&lt;/code&gt; in an SVG animation tool and an HTML-to-Pug converter, and &lt;code&gt;URL.createObjectURL&lt;/code&gt; in an SVG viewer. That last one is a good example of why this can hide: on the server it produced nothing useful, in the browser it produced a &lt;code&gt;blob:&lt;/code&gt; URL, and the visible symptom was a broken image, not an error.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Wrong: runs during render on the server too.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;src&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;URL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;createObjectURL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Blob&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;([&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;svg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;image/svg+xml&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The fix is to compute these only after the component has mounted:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useHasMounted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;mounted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setMounted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setMounted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[]);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;mounted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;mounted&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useHasMounted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;src&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useMemo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;mounted&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;URL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;createObjectURL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Blob&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;([&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;svg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;image/svg+xml&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}))&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;mounted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;svg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Remember to revoke the URL when nothing can reach it any more, but that is a separate bug.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


4. &lt;code&gt;usePathname()&lt;/code&gt; in a prerendered component
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the one that cost the most, because I fixed it and then shipped it again.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The site's navigation highlights the current page. The highlight compared the path:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pathname&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;usePathname&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;isActive&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pathname&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;href&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;On the deployed homepage this logged #418 on every visit. It never happened locally, with &lt;code&gt;next dev&lt;/code&gt; or with a local production build. The comparison for the &lt;code&gt;/&lt;/code&gt; link came out differently in Vercel's prerender of the index route than in the browser, so the active class differed between the server HTML and the first client render.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first time, I fixed it in one header. Later a different navigation design carried the same comparison into a new component, and it went live again. That second time is the one that took Lighthouse from 96 to 83.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fix that stuck is a shared hook that gates only the &lt;code&gt;/&lt;/code&gt; comparison on mount and leaves every other link server-rendered, so the other routes keep their active state from the first paint. Simplified, it looks like this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useIsRouteActive&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;href&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pathname&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;usePathname&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;mounted&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useHasMounted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;href&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;mounted&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pathname&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pathname&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;href&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pathname&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;startsWith&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;href&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;/`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The second part of the fix was making sure it could not come back. The repository has a small script that runs before every build and fails when a client component compares &lt;code&gt;usePathname()&lt;/code&gt; directly. A lint rule would do the same job. Writing the lesson down had not been enough.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


5. A script that edits &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;body&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; before React gets there
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The site sets a colour scheme and, on festival days, a few &lt;code&gt;data-&lt;/code&gt; attributes on &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;body&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; from an inline script, so the right look paints before any JavaScript bundle loads. React then hydrates &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;body&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;, finds attributes it did not render, and complains.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the one case where &lt;code&gt;suppressHydrationWarning&lt;/code&gt; is the right tool:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;suppressHydrationWarning&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;It only silences attribute and text differences on that one element, not its children, which is exactly the scope you want. Two rules keep it honest: only the element the script edits gets it (here &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;body&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;, not &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;html&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;), and the script only ever sets attributes, never content.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;suppressHydrationWarning&lt;/code&gt; is not a fix for the first four causes. Those are real differences in what renders, and hiding the warning leaves the re-render in place.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How to find yours
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Read the console on the deployed site,&lt;/strong&gt; not just locally. Three of these five never reproduced on my machine.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Run the development build once&lt;/strong&gt; to get the readable message and the diff React prints, which names the element and the two values.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Load the page with a foreign locale and time zone.&lt;/strong&gt; It finds cause 2 and anything else that reads the environment.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Search the codebase&lt;/strong&gt; for &lt;code&gt;Math.random&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Date.now&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;new Date(&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;toLocale&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;window.&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;document.&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;DOMParser&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;usePathname&lt;/code&gt; inside components that render on the server.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The common thread is simple: everything a component renders the first time must be computable on the server, from the same inputs, with the same answer. Anything that depends on the browser, the clock, chance or the visitor waits until after mount.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A Next.js app in a container needs one setting most tutorials skip: &lt;code&gt;output: "standalone"&lt;/code&gt;, which trims the build down to only the files &lt;code&gt;next start&lt;/code&gt; actually needs instead of the full &lt;code&gt;node_modules&lt;/code&gt; tree. Get that right and the rest of a Next.js deploy on Ownkube is a normal git push.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This walks through deploying a Next.js app (App Router or Pages Router, both work) on Ownkube Compute: the standalone build, environment variables split between build time and runtime, an optional managed Postgres for apps using an ORM, and automatic TLS.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;TL;DR&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Set &lt;code&gt;output: "standalone"&lt;/code&gt; in &lt;code&gt;next.config.js&lt;/code&gt; so the Docker image ships a minimal server instead of the whole &lt;code&gt;node_modules&lt;/code&gt; folder.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Push the app with a &lt;code&gt;Dockerfile&lt;/code&gt;, and Ownkube builds it, deploys it, and issues TLS automatically on your &lt;code&gt;*.ownkube.app&lt;/code&gt; hostname.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;NEXT_PUBLIC_*&lt;/code&gt; variables get baked into the build; everything else is a runtime environment variable set in the dashboard.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Add an Ownkube-managed Postgres database if you use Prisma or another ORM, and point the connection string at it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Turn on horizontal autoscaling once traffic is uneven, since Next.js's SSR path is CPU-bound per request in a way static pages are not.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


1. Enable standalone output
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In &lt;code&gt;next.config.js&lt;/code&gt;, add the &lt;a href="https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/config/next-config-js/output" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;standalone output mode&lt;/a&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// next.config.js&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="cm"&gt;/** @type {import('next').NextConfig} */&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;nextConfig&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;output&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;standalone&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nx"&gt;module&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;exports&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;nextConfig&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This changes what &lt;code&gt;next build&lt;/code&gt; produces. It emits a self-contained &lt;code&gt;.next/standalone&lt;/code&gt; folder with only the files the server needs, plus a minimal &lt;code&gt;server.js&lt;/code&gt; entry point, so the runtime no longer needs the full project and &lt;code&gt;node_modules&lt;/code&gt;. That keeps the final Docker image small.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


2. Write a Dockerfile
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A &lt;a href="https://docs.docker.com/build/building/multi-stage/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;multi-stage build&lt;/a&gt; keeps dependencies out of the final image:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight docker"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;node:20-slim&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;AS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;deps&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;WORKDIR&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt; /app&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;COPY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt; package.json package-lock.json ./&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;RUN &lt;/span&gt;npm ci

&lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;node:20-slim&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;AS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;build&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;WORKDIR&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt; /app&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;COPY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt; --from=deps /app/node_modules ./node_modules&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;COPY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt; . .&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# NEXT_PUBLIC_* values must be present at build time; they end up in the client bundle&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;ARG&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt; NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;ENV&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt; NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=$NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;RUN &lt;/span&gt;npm run build

&lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt; node:20-slim&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;WORKDIR&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt; /app&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;ENV&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt; NODE_ENV=production&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;COPY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt; --from=build /app/.next/standalone ./&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;COPY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt; --from=build /app/.next/static ./.next/static&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;COPY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt; --from=build /app/public ./public&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;EXPOSE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt; 3000&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;CMD&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt; ["node", "server.js"]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The build stage takes &lt;code&gt;NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL&lt;/code&gt; as a build argument because Next.js inlines &lt;code&gt;NEXT_PUBLIC_*&lt;/code&gt; variables into the client JavaScript at build time. A value set only at runtime in the dashboard will not reach client-side code; it has to be present when &lt;code&gt;npm run build&lt;/code&gt; runs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


3. Push the repo and create the app
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Connect the repository from the Ownkube dashboard (&lt;code&gt;app.ownkube.io&lt;/code&gt;) to deploy on every push, or deploy the current directory with the &lt;code&gt;okctl&lt;/code&gt; CLI:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;okctl login
okctl up &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--name&lt;/span&gt; nextjs-app &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--port&lt;/span&gt; 3000
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Ownkube builds the image, allocates a hostname on &lt;code&gt;*.ownkube.app&lt;/code&gt;, and issues TLS automatically, so there is no reverse proxy or certificate to set up by hand.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


4. Set your environment variables
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Split variables by when Next.js actually needs them:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c"&gt;# Server-only, read at runtime by API routes or Server Components&lt;/span&gt;
okctl vars &lt;span class="nb"&gt;set &lt;/span&gt;nextjs-app &lt;span class="nv"&gt;AUTH_SECRET&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;$(&lt;/span&gt;openssl rand &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-base64&lt;/span&gt; 32&lt;span class="si"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--secret&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c"&gt;# Client-visible, baked into the bundle at build time, so set it as a build argument (see step 2)&lt;/span&gt;
okctl deploy build-args nextjs-app &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--arg&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://nextjs-app.ownkube.app/api"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Environment variables set through Ownkube are encrypted at rest and only injected into the running container, which is why a &lt;code&gt;NEXT_PUBLIC_&lt;/code&gt; value goes in as a build argument instead. Anything without the &lt;code&gt;NEXT_PUBLIC_&lt;/code&gt; prefix stays server-side and never ships to the browser.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


5. Add a managed Postgres database, if you use an ORM
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A Next.js app using &lt;a href="https://www.prisma.io/docs/orm/prisma-migrate" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Prisma&lt;/a&gt;, Drizzle, or another ORM for a database-backed API route needs a real Postgres instance. Create a database box from the dashboard: &lt;strong&gt;New &amp;gt; Database &amp;gt; Postgres&lt;/strong&gt;, walked through in full in &lt;a href="https://ownkube.io/blog/add-managed-postgres-database" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;add a managed Postgres database to your app&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It provisions a private (in-region) Postgres box starting at $4 a month plus storage at $0.25 per GB, with backups and point-in-time recovery available on Personal and Team. The database runs as a single instance by default; you can turn on opt-in public access and, on Team, high availability (a primary plus up to two standby instances) per database when you need them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Link the database to the app, and Ownkube writes its connection string into the app as a secret &lt;code&gt;DATABASE_URL&lt;/code&gt; variable and redeploys it:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;okctl deploy &lt;span class="nb"&gt;link &lt;/span&gt;nextjs-app &amp;lt;database-id&amp;gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;With Prisma, generate the client in the build stage:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight docker"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c"&gt;# In the build stage, after copying the schema&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;RUN &lt;/span&gt;npx prisma generate
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Run &lt;code&gt;prisma migrate deploy&lt;/code&gt; as a one-off &lt;a href="https://ownkube.io/docs/deployments/jobs" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;job&lt;/a&gt; instead of in the build. Variables reach the running container rather than the build, and the database is private to its region, so the build cannot reach it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The slim standalone image leaves out the Prisma CLI and your &lt;code&gt;prisma&lt;/code&gt; folder, so give the job its own Dockerfile that installs dependencies and copies the schema, set its command to &lt;code&gt;npx prisma migrate deploy&lt;/code&gt;, and link the same database to it. Then trigger it when a release includes new migrations:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;okctl deploy &lt;span class="nb"&gt;link &lt;/span&gt;nextjs-migrate &amp;lt;database-id&amp;gt;
okctl deploy job-runs trigger nextjs-migrate
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;A purely static or content-fetching Next.js app can skip a database entirely.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


6. Scale and watch it run
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A fresh Next.js app usually starts fine on a small metered ceiling, for example a 500m CPU limit and 512 MiB of memory, set on the sliders under &lt;strong&gt;Settings → Resources&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Turn on &lt;a href="https://ownkube.io/blog/autoscale-cpu-memory" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;horizontal autoscaling&lt;/a&gt; if traffic is uneven, so Ownkube adds server instances under load and scales back down when it is quiet. On Ownkube Compute you turn it on through your coding agent (the &lt;code&gt;update_deployment&lt;/code&gt; MCP tool) or the API, with a minimum and maximum replica count and a CPU target such as 70%.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Server-side rendering and API routes are CPU work per request in a way a purely static export is not, so an SSR-heavy app benefits more from autoscaling headroom than a mostly-static one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Metered billing charges CPU and memory on actual per-minute usage, so a quiet Next.js app between visits barely moves the wallet. Logs and metrics for the app live in the dashboard, so a slow render or a route that keeps erroring shows up in one place.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Moving to your own AWS later
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the app needs to run inside your own AWS account later, for compliance reasons or because a client wants their name on the infrastructure, the same Dockerfile and Next.js app move over with no rewrite. Ownkube provisions the instance, wires up the same deploy flow, and brings the logs, metrics, and health checks with it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Railway only runs your app on its own infrastructure. Our &lt;a href="https://ownkube.io/compare/railway-alternatives" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Railway alternatives guide&lt;/a&gt; compares Railway with the other options.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


FAQ
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Do I need &lt;code&gt;output: "standalone"&lt;/code&gt;, or does the default build work?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The default build works, but it expects the full project and &lt;code&gt;node_modules&lt;/code&gt; present at runtime, which makes for a much larger Docker image and a slower deploy. Standalone output is the setting almost every Next.js Docker guide converges on, and it is the one used in the Dockerfile above.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Does Ownkube support the App Router and Server Actions?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes, both the App Router and the older Pages Router run the same way, since &lt;code&gt;next start&lt;/code&gt; in the standalone build serves whatever the app builds. Server Actions run as part of the same Node process and read server-side environment variables normally.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


How do I handle a value that is different between preview and production?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Set the environment variable per app in the dashboard or with &lt;code&gt;okctl vars set &amp;lt;app&amp;gt; NAME=VALUE&lt;/code&gt;, and use a separate Ownkube app for each environment you want isolated, each with its own hostname, environment variables, and (if needed) database.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


What happens if my wallet balance runs out mid-month?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The app pauses and any attached database keeps its data. Adding credit or the next monthly plan load resumes it. Unused wallet credit rolls over and never expires.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Can I use a custom domain?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An automatic &lt;code&gt;*.ownkube.app&lt;/code&gt; hostname with TLS is provisioned for every app the moment it deploys, so you always have a working URL with no configuration. On Personal and Team you can also add your own domain to the app. You set two DNS records and Ownkube issues and renews the certificate. See &lt;a href="https://ownkube.io/docs/networking/custom-domains" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Custom domains&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Should I host Next.js on Vercel instead?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a personal, non-commercial frontend, Vercel's Hobby plan is free and runs Next.js well. A commercial project needs Pro at $20 per developer seat a month. &lt;a href="https://ownkube.io/compare/is-vercel-free" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Is Vercel free?&lt;/a&gt; covers the Hobby rules. Ownkube fits when the app needs a long-running server, workers or Postgres next to it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For the always-current version of this walkthrough, see the &lt;a href="https://ownkube.io/docs/deploy/nextjs" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Deploy Next.js&lt;/a&gt; guide in the docs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Where Ownkube fits
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Next.js wants a slim server image, a clean split between build-time and runtime configuration, and a database only when the app actually uses one, and Ownkube gives you all three without provisioning a cloud account first. Set standalone output, push the Dockerfile, set your environment variables, and the hostname and TLS are already waiting. See &lt;a href="https://ownkube.io/pricing" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Ownkube pricing&lt;/a&gt; for the per-minute wallet model, from $5 a month. &lt;a href="https://app.ownkube.io/login" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Deploy my first app&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
